nce/decode_loop/dprof_ctx_1p3_on.txt`).** This is the steering\n924:      **All 16 legs faster, all 16 byte-identical in the emitted text, `tau` and mean verify width\n1047:      `build/decode` is argmax, unseeded, single-stream, no HTTP, and its width controller reads the\n1049:      — so \"the faster arm picked a different width\" does **not** explain it. Yet two runs of the\n1064:      byte-identical text**, with `tau` and mean verify width matching to four decimals. So at 16\n1421:      **16 of 16 legs emitted a byte-identical `text_sha256`, with `tau` and mean verify width equal\n1478:      hpb=1 at that prefill, and 0.52x at the K=6 verify width.** The number was never re-measured\n1538:      correlates with width by construction and the excess must be read at fixed width — rule 0.3):\n1567:        **9/9 legs byte-identical**, `tau` and realised width equal to three decimals on every leg —\n1662:      points (realised width 2.56 -> 2.74, P(boundary) ~ w/4). The other reading is that the two\n1852:      break-even ctx 6,471.** 16 of 16 legs byte-identical, `tau` and mean verify width equal on\n1900:      with the width term controlled give `b` 0.924 -> 0.381 and `a` 82.83 -> 85.74 -- the same\n1905:      `KV_PRECISION_FINDINGS.md` §4 said in advance that this is not a bandwidth-bound path and\n1960:- [ ] **2.1** Re-tune block width AFTER 1.1/1.2. Verify width is currently free only by accident of\n2099:      **1575 MHz is worth nothing, which is what a bandwidth-bound engine should say about a core\n2138:**Authorised 2026-08-20.** The kernel path is at the bandwidth floor and `tau` is the only\n2179:      corpus; online it drives block width (P2.6). If they are two implementations they will\n2252:- [ ] **P2.6** **HASS on draft steps 2–3, and pattern-gated block width.** Two changes, sequenced\n2257:      (b) **Block width gated on the P2.1 signal.** The static sweep is done and pooled: F93/F94\n2265:      **block 40**. If width moves materially, **re-price `m16` — do not assume it stayed dead.**\n2266:      **Gate: pattern-gated width beats static block 6 on the suite, outside the 3.5 % spread.**","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"9d0b6f31-0c0c-4fa3-a8f4-6a7b332bf789","uuid":"cc301199-3ecb-462c-a25c-9e35b37b0fa2","timestamp":"2026-08-21T07:08:42.305Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"327:      | ctx | ms/forward | spread | tau | mean verify width | tok/s |\n337:      **The slope is not a speculation artifact.** `tau` and realised verify width both correlate\n338:      +0.35 with context here, and width is what sets bytes per forward, so the raw slope could in\n340:      `fwd = 70.74 + 7.029 x (ctx/1000) + 23.04 x width` (R^2 0.985, SE 0.145 and 3.50). Width does\n342:      **7.03 +/- 0.28** with width held fixed.\n389:      the realised verify width fixed, because width sets bytes-per-forward and correlates with\n405:      measuring the right thing.** 0.3's wall-clock fit: `b = 7.362 +/- 0.370`, width-controlled\n406:      **7.029 +/- 0.28**. This run's dprof step total: `6.488 +/- 0.332`, width-controlled\n428:      **What is NOT the context term.** MoE is flat (`-0.079 +/- 0.029` per 1000 with width fixed);\n429:      its apparent -0.38 uncontrolled is a width artifact and disappears when width is held. Every\n452:      and the same context**. A 3.2x swing in a GEMM at fixed shape is not explained by width or by\n474:      every point at ctx >= 1536 both arms produced **bit-identical tau and mean verify width\n486:      Bands are **+/-1 %** against the 3.5 % run-to-run spread, because pairing removes verify-width\n491:      same-arm cohort of 165.7-176.5 at identical tau and width, and its *before* twin (216.55) is\n568:      (width held fixed). 0.2's 0.10 ms was real but taken at **context 9** — 135x low.\n596:      35 of 52 legs paired exactly (identical `tau` AND identical mean verify width rep-for-rep); all\n610:      verify-width variance rather than averaging over it.\n614:      width-control